# Line endings, pinned rather than left to each developer's git config.
#
# Git for Windows offers `core.autocrlf=true` as its *default* install option.
# Without this file every text file in the repository is converted on checkout
# there, and the shell scripts stop working the moment they are run under Git
# Bash, WSL or a container: `/usr/bin/env bash^M: bad interpreter`. Since
# scripts/install.sh invokes its siblings directly, one CRLF checkout breaks the
# whole documented onboarding path -- and a Windows developer who then edits and
# commits a script can break it for Linux and macOS colleagues too.
#
# The scripts are POSIX (spec/constitution.md, Article XII, and spec/design/conventions.md § Scripts), so a
# CRLF checkout does not degrade them -- it breaks them. This file is what keeps
# that from depending on a colleague's git config; CI enforces the rest.

# Normalise everything to LF in the repository, and check out LF everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- Executable text, where the ending is load-bearing ---------------------
*.sh        text eol=lf

# --- Test data, compared byte for byte -------------------------------------
# The reference corpus is JSON under golden-set/ and is read by a parser that
# tolerates either ending; nothing here compares its bytes. The CSV and cp1250
# fixtures a previous product kept are gone, and so are their rules -- a rule
# for a file that does not exist reads as a promise nobody can check.

# --- Archived session transcripts, compared by hash ------------------------
# `-text` is the load-bearing half: `* text=auto eol=lf` would normalise CR
# bytes on commit, so the sha256
# that `retro_capture.py` records for the working-tree file would not match the
# bytes a fresh clone gets, and `verify` would fail on every machine except the
# one that captured. `-diff` because these are multi-megabyte JSONL blobs whose
# textual diff is noise nobody reads.
spec/changes/*/sessions/**   -text -diff linguist-generated

# The session corpus: every session that is NOT a change's own. Same rule, same
# reason -- `corpus.py verify` compares sha256 against the working-tree bytes, so a
# normalising checkout would make every archive report damage on every machine
# except the one that captured it. `INDEX.md` is excluded below because it is
# generated prose a reviewer reads, not bytes compared by hash.
retro/corpus/**              -text -diff linguist-generated
retro/corpus/INDEX.md        text eol=lf linguist-generated

# --- Binary ----------------------------------------------------------------
# No binary file is tracked today, so no rule stands here: the rules this section
# used to carry (`*.xlsx`, `*.pdf`, `*.png`, `*.woff2`, ...) matched nothing, and
# the standard three sections above give is that a rule for a file that does not
# exist reads as a promise nobody can check. When a binary fixture arrives, name
# its extension here as `binary` in the same commit, so `text=auto` never guesses.

# --- Generated, so diffs are noise -----------------------------------------
uv.lock                        -diff linguist-generated
frontend/package-lock.json     -diff linguist-generated
frontend/src/api/schema.d.ts   linguist-generated

# The change registry: one entry per change, projected from that change's own
# `change.json`. Marked generated so a reviewer reads the record rather than the
# view of it.
#
# Deliberately NOT `merge=ours`. Git ships no built-in `ours` driver: the
# attribute only works where somebody has run `git config merge.ours.driver true`,
# so it would silently fall back to a text merge on a colleague's clone -- the
# failure arriving through a config step instead of a file format. Conflicts here
# are resolved by regenerating, which is one command and needs no git verbs:
#     sdd-engine gen_indexes
spec/changes/status.json       -diff linguist-generated
